findings.body:
The new formatter, added in this PR to go with the new Studies copy, formats `strings.detailTextStudies`. That is the old "%@ may install and run studies from time to time." string. The new `detailTextStudiesV2` ("%@ randomly selects users to test features…") has no caller anywhere.

::diff{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="417-430"}

`formatRolloutsText` correctly uses the new `detailTextRollouts`, so the two methods disagree about which copy generation they serve. Anyone wiring this up in the follow-up PR will show the outdated Studies text next to the new "Allow Feature Studies" label (`labelStudiesV2`).

::suggestion{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="423-425"}
        func formatStudiesText(appName: String) -> String {
            return String(format: strings.detailTextStudiesV2, appName)
        }

It's also worth asking whether this singleton should exist at all. `SettingsViewController.swift:127` already calls `String(format: UIConstants.strings.detailTextStudies, AppInfo.productName)` inline. A strings-only PR shouldn't add an unused helper class with private-init/singleton overhead.

`Settings.detailTextSendUsageData.v2` has exactly the same value and comment as the existing `Settings.detailTextSendUsageData`. It has nothing to do with Studies or Rollouts, and nothing uses it.

::diff{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="202-203"}

A new key with unchanged text makes every Focus locale re-translate the same sentence for no user-visible benefit. You normally bump a key version only when the source text changes. I'd drop it.

The comment tells translators the placeholder is `%1$@`, but the source value uses `%@`. The en-CA/en-GB `.strings` comments in this same PR say `%@`, so the Swift source and the generated files disagree.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="6257-6261"}

Translators copy placeholders from the comment, so a mismatch like this can produce translations that fail the placeables check or render oddly.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="6261-6261"}
        comment: "A short description that explains that Mozilla may change features remotely. %@ is the app name (e.g. Firefox).")

This PR adds `Settings.Studies.Message.v148` to the quotes-linter exclusions, but its text ("%@ randomly selects users to test features, which improves quality for everyone.") has no quote characters in it.

::diff{file=".github/l10n/linter_config_ios.json" lines="7-11"}

The exclusion does nothing today. Worse, it would let a future revision of this key sneak in straight quotes without the linter catching it. If you added it to silence a linter failure, that failure probably came from a different key. Remove it, or explain why it's needed.

These locale files are normally brought in from the l10n repository by the import automation, not edited in feature PRs. Hand-added entries will be overwritten, or will cause churn, on the next import. Also, the en-GB file's Learn More entries keep "Learn More" instead of British casing and style, so these may not match what en-GB localizers would choose.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Supporting Files/en-GB.lproj/Settings.strings" lines="238-260"}

The en-GB diff also drops the trailing blank line at the end of the file, which is unrelated churn.
